{"comments": [{"path": "client/scripts/game/charts/ChartCatalog.cs", "existing_code": "        var roots = IsEditorOrInternal
            ? new[] { "res://testdata/packs", "user://charts" }
            : new[] { "user://charts" };
        foreach (var root in roots)
        {
            using var dir = DirAccess.Open(root);
            if (dir == null)
                continue;", "content": "`using var dir` inside the loop is disposed only at the end of the enclosing method scope (C# `using var` is scope-bound, including on `continue` it is still fine), but the more important problem is silent failure: when `DirAccess.Open` returns null (root missing, unreadable, or `user://charts` never created on a fresh install) the whole root is skipped without any diagnostic. A misconfigured content directory therefore looks identical to "no packs installed", which is very hard to debug in the field. Also `sub` names are used unchecked — a directory like `..` (or a name containing separators) would be interpolated into `$"{root}/{sub}"` and could resolve outside `root`; `ChartPack.Load` only validates paths *inside* meta.json, not the pack directory itself. Consider logging the open failure and skipping non-plain directory names.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": "        foreach (var root in roots)
        {
            using var dir = DirAccess.Open(root);
            if (dir == null)
            {
                GD.PushWarning($"ChartCatalog: cannot open chart root '{root}'");
                continue;
            }
            foreach (var sub in dir.GetDirectories())
            {
                if (sub.Length == 0 || sub is "." or ".." || sub.Contains('/') || sub.Contains('\\'))
                {
                    GD.PushWarning($"ChartCatalog: skipping illegal directory name '{sub}' in '{root}'");
                    continue;
                }"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/charts/ChartCatalog.cs", "existing_code": "                var pack = ChartPack.Load($"{root}/{sub}");
                if (pack != null)
                    byId[pack.Id] = pack;", "content": "Two related issues:
1. `ChartPack.Load` returning null (missing/invalid meta.json, parse failure) is silently dropped — since `Load` already logs a warning internally this is tolerable, but nothing reports *which* package was skipped at catalog level; acceptable but worth noting.
2. Override semantics: because dev packs load first and `user://charts` second, any user pack with the same `Id` silently shadows trusted `res://testdata/packs` content, with no warning. In internal/editor builds a user-supplied pack can thus replace test data used for validation. Per the comment this is intentional, but it deserves at least a `GD.PushWarning`/`PushError` on collision so shadowing is observable rather than invisible. Also note `pack.Id` is unvalidated (empty string allowed) — an empty/duplicate id would collapse multiple packs into one entry.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": "                var pack = ChartPack.Load($"{root}/{sub}");
                if (pack == null)
                    continue;
                if (pack.Id.Length == 0)
                {
                    GD.PushWarning($"ChartCatalog: package at '{root}/{sub}' has an empty id");
                    continue;
                }
                if (byId.ContainsKey(pack.Id))
                    GD.PushWarning($"ChartCatalog: package '{pack.Id}' from '{root}' overrides " +
                        $"previously loaded package '{byId[pack.Id].DirPath}'");
                byId[pack.Id] = pack;"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/charts/ChartCatalog.cs", "existing_code": "    public static bool IsEditorOrInternal => OS.HasFeature("internal_testdata") ||
        OS.HasFeature("editor");", "content": "`OS.HasFeature` performs a string lookup in the engine's feature tag set on every access, and this property is re-evaluated for each `ScanAll` call (and publicly exposed via `ChartPack.IsEditorOrInternal`). It is cheap but trivially cacheable; more importantly, the gating should be documented — a shipped build is only safe from `res://testdata/packs` if `internal_testdata` is a custom feature that is never included in release export presets. There is no enforcement/assert here, so a mistakenly-enabled feature flag silently ships test data. Consider a static readonly cached field and a comment naming the export preset requirement.", "category": "security", "severity": "low", "suggestion_code": "    // Enabled only when the 'internal_testdata' custom feature is set (internal QA builds) or in
    // the editor. Release export presets must never declare 'internal_testdata'.
    public static bool IsEditorOrInternal { get; } =
        OS.HasFeature("internal_testdata") || OS.HasFeature("editor");"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/charts/ChartCatalog.cs", "existing_code": "        return byId.Values.OrderBy(pack => pack.Title).ToList();", "content": "`ScanAll` does a full synchronous disk scan and JSON parse of every package on each call (called from `GameSession` at line 37, and via `ChartPack.ScanAll`), with no caching or invalidation. On a device with many user packs this is repeated expensive I/O and parsing on every session/start. Also, `OrderBy(pack => pack.Title)` uses the current culture comparer, so ordering can differ between locales; if list ordering must be stable (e.g. for index-based UI or persisted selections) use an ordinal/culture-invariant comparer.", "category": "performance", "severity": "low", "suggestion_code": "        return byId.Values
            .OrderBy(pack => pack.Title, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            .ToList();"}]}

{"comments": [{"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "        var loaded = path.StartsWith("res://", StringComparison.Ordinal)
            ? WithVirtualFileAccess(Load)
            : Load();
        if (loaded != null && cacheable)
        {
            lock (AudioLock)
                AudioCache.Set(path, loaded);
        }
        return loaded;", "content": "Deadlock risk: the virtual-file lock is explicitly re-entrant usage (V2Integration calls WithVirtualFileAccess -> Godot.FileAccess, and any nested Res call would take the same non-recursive lock). `lock` on a plain object is re-entrant for the *same* thread, so nesting is fine, but there is a real problem: `WithVirtualFileAccess(Load)` holds the process-wide lock for the entire decode of the res:// file (`GD.Load` / `AudioStreamWav.LoadFromFile` / `Image.LoadFromFile` can do full disk IO + decode). Since V2Integration also wraps chart parsing in the same lock, a large audio decode blocks chart loads and vice-versa. Also note the named local function `Load` shadows nothing but is captured fine — however `AudioCache.Set` is done outside the same lock as the `TryGet` (double-load race): two threads loading the same non-res path can both decode and then both `Set`, and the last writer's stream wins while the first caller keeps a stream that is no longer in the cache. Not fatal for AudioStream, but wasteful (double decode) and inconsistent with the "cached instance" intent. Consider holding AudioLock for the whole load or documenting the duplication.", "category": "performance", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": ""}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "        if (!TryReadRawImageDimensions(extension, bytes, out var headerWidth, out var headerHeight) ||
            !DimensionsWithinLimits(headerWidth, headerHeight))", "content": "The 8 MB byte cap does not actually bound *decode* memory for compressed formats: an 8 MB PNG/WebP of a few thousand pixels can legitimately expand to huge bitmaps, and the header parse is the only pre-decode guard. That guard is bypassable in practice because the parsers only recognize a narrow set of variants:
- PNG: requires `bytes[12..16] == "IHDR"` at offset 12, which is only true when the first chunk is IHDR (it usually is, but a PNG with a leading ancillary chunk would be rejected) — header parse failure silently drops valid images.
- WebP: requires `bytes.Length >= 30` before dispatch; a valid short VP8L file (< 30 bytes) fails even though the VP8L branch only needs 25 bytes; also the `bytes.Length >= 30` check for `VP8 ` (lossy) is checked after the top-level `bytes.Length < 30` so the inner re-check is redundant.
Consider making the length checks per-branch (VP8X needs 30, VP8L needs 25, VP8 needs 30) so valid small files aren't dropped, and keeping the post-decode `DimensionsWithinLimits` as the authoritative check (it is present and correct).", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": "        if (bytes.Length < 16 || !bytes.AsSpan(0, 4).SequenceEqual("RIFF"u8) ||
            !bytes.AsSpan(8, 4).SequenceEqual("WEBP"u8))
            return false;

        var chunk = bytes.AsSpan(12, 4);
        if (chunk.SequenceEqual("VP8X"u8))
        {
            if (bytes.Length < 30) return false;
            width = 1 + ReadLittleEndian24(bytes, 24);
            height = 1 + ReadLittleEndian24(bytes, 27);
            return true;
        }
        if (chunk.SequenceEqual("VP8L"u8))
        {
            if (bytes.Length < 25 || bytes[20] != 0x2f) return false;
            var packed = bytes[21] | (bytes[22] << 8) | (bytes[23] << 16) | (bytes[24] << 24);
            width = (packed & 0x3fff) + 1;
            height = ((packed >> 14) & 0x3fff) + 1;
            return true;
        }
        if (chunk.SequenceEqual("VP8 "u8))
        {
            if (bytes.Length < 30 || bytes[23] != 0x9d || bytes[24] != 0x01 || bytes[25] != 0x2a) return false;
            width = (bytes[26] | (bytes[27] << 8)) & 0x3fff;
            height = (bytes[28] | (bytes[29] << 8)) & 0x3fff;
            return true;
        }
        return false;"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "        var offset = 2;
        while (offset + 3 < bytes.Length)
        {
            if (bytes[offset++] != 0xff)
                continue;
            while (offset < bytes.Length && bytes[offset] == 0xff)
                offset++;
            if (offset >= bytes.Length)
                return false;
            var marker = bytes[offset++];
            if (marker is 0xd8 or 0xd9 || marker is >= 0xd0 and <= 0xd7)
                continue;
            if (offset + 1 >= bytes.Length)
                return false;
            var length = (bytes[offset] << 8) | bytes[offset + 1];
            if (length < 2 || offset + length > bytes.Length)
                return false;
            if (marker is >= 0xc0 and <= 0xc3 or >= 0xc5 and <= 0xc7 or
                >= 0xc9 and <= 0xcb or >= 0xcd and <= 0xcf)
            {
                if (length < 7)
                    return false;
                height = (bytes[offset + 3] << 8) | bytes[offset + 4];
                width = (bytes[offset + 5] << 8) | bytes[offset + 6];
                return true;
            }
            offset += length;
        }
        return false;", "content": "JPEG parser has a subtle correctness bug: `length` (the segment length word, big-endian) INCLUDES the two length bytes themselves, and `offset` currently points at the length word. The SOF dimension reads `bytes[offset + 3..6]` correctly assume that, but `offset += length` is correct too — so that part is fine. The real issue is the bounds test `offset + length > bytes.Length`: with `length` up to 0xFFFF and `offset` up to `int.MaxValue`-ish this cannot overflow for files under 8 MB, but the *last* valid segment may end exactly at `bytes.Length`, which passes, and then the loop condition `offset + 3 < bytes.Length` correctly stops. So no overflow here.

What is actually wrong:
1. SOS (0xDA) is not handled: the scan should stop at SOF, but if a file has no SOF before SOS (progressive/malformed), continuing to scan into entropy-coded data will misinterpret compressed bytes as segment markers, and `offset += length` can jump arbitrarily. It is bounded by the `return false` and length check, so it's not a hang, but the parse is unreliable.
2. `width`/`height` read as `bytes[offset + 3] << 8 | bytes[offset + 4]` etc. — fine, but `offset + 6` is never verified beyond `length >= 7` (length>=7 means offset..offset+6 are inside the segment which is inside the buffer), OK.
3. SOF markers `0xC4` (DHT), `0xC8` (JPG) and `0xCC` (DAC) are correctly excluded.

Recommendation: explicitly `if (marker == 0xDA) return false;` before the length read so scanning never enters scan data, which makes the parser deterministic for progressive JPEGs whose SOF appears after DHTs.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": "            var marker = bytes[offset++];
            if (marker is 0xd8 or 0xd9 || marker is >= 0xd0 and <= 0xd7)
                continue;
            if (marker == 0xda) // start of scan: no SOF found, give up
                return false;
            if (offset + 1 >= bytes.Length)
                return false;"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "    public static Texture2D? LoadTexture(string path)
    {", "content": "`LoadTexture` has no cache (unlike `LoadAudio`), so every call decodes the file from disk and allocates a new `ImageBuffer`/GPU texture. Callers that reload the same bitmap (e.g. per-chart cover art, icons during a UI refresh) will churn GPU memory and cause repeated disk IO; Godot's `ImageTexture` also duplicates on the GPU. Please confirm this is intentional — if not, mirror the AudioCache approach with a bounded texture cache (and evict on chart change), since the per-call cost here is much higher than for audio.", "category": "performance", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": ""}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "            if (!Godot.FileAccess.FileExists(path))
            {
                GD.PushWarning($"Res.LoadTexture: 文件不存在 {path}");
                return null;
            }
            var trustedImage = Image.LoadFromFile(path);", "content": "The `res://` branch is labeled "trusted" but that is not guaranteed: in an exported/modded build, or when `res://` maps to a writable path in a development/PCK-less launch, a file could be attacker-influenced, and `Image.LoadFromFile` decodes with NO byte cap and NO dimension cap (a decompression bomb here is unconstrained). The `res://` path also silently accepts `.bmp`, `.tga`, `.svg` etc. that the raw branch rejects. If `res://` contents are genuinely trusted this is acceptable, but the asymmetry should be documented (the code only says "Trusted project resources"). If there is any chance of a writable/mounted `res://` (e.g. mobile expansions, `--path` overrides), apply the same MaxRawTextureBytes/limit checks by routing raw (non-`.import`ed) files through the guarded path.", "category": "security", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": ""}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "            if (path.StartsWith("res://", StringComparison.Ordinal) && ResourceLoader.Exists(path))
                return GD.Load<AudioStream>(path);", "content": "`GD.Load<AudioStream>(path)` is a miss-tolerant but uncached (in this class) call; Godot caches loaded resources internally keyed by path. More importantly, when `ResourceLoader.Exists(path)` returns true but `GD.Load` returns null (corrupt import), this returns null with no error logged, whereas the fallback path does `GD.PushError`. Add a warning so a missing audio asset is diagnosable rather than silently silent.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "low", "suggestion_code": "            if (path.StartsWith("res://", StringComparison.Ordinal) && ResourceLoader.Exists(path))
            {
                var res = GD.Load<AudioStream>(path);
                if (res == null)
                    GD.PushWarning($"Res.LoadAudio: 无法加载已导入音频 {path}");
                return res;
            }"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/common/Res.cs", "existing_code": "        if (Api.NullOrEmpty is not null) { }", "content": "No test hints: this file contains hand-written binary parsers (PNG/JPEG/WebP) plus limits logic, which are exactly the boundary cases that need coverage. There is no unit test project reference for `Res`/`TryReadRawImageDimensions` in the repo despite `tools/core-tests` existing. Please add tests for: truncated headers at exactly the declared minimum lengths, JPEG with DHT before SOF, JPEG with no SOF, WebP VP8L < 30 bytes, dimensions at exactly MaxRawTextureWidth/Height/Pixels, and a file whose header claims small dimensions but decodes large (post-decode guard).", "category": "test", "severity": "low", "suggestion_code": ""}]}

{"comments": [{"path": "client/scripts/game/gameplay/GameplayMain.Mixer.cs", "existing_code": "		var phase = input.Action == PointerAction.Press || !_mixerTrackPointers.Contains(input.Id)
			? ContactPhase.Began : ContactPhase.Moved;", "content": "`_mixerTrackPointers` is seeded only from `_pointerBuffer.InitialTouches` (pointers already down on this track at frame start) and then updated as the frame's inputs are replayed. Two consequences:

1. A `Press` that does not project onto this track still yields `ContactPhase.Began` (action is Press). `MixerRuntime.Apply` stores `_touches[id]` *before* the geometry check (MixerRuntime.cs:91-94), so a finger that lands elsewhere but happens to be inside this path's bounds can be treated as a *began* contact and become the controller (`Placed = true, Controller = id`). For other tracks the seed list contains these pointers, so they correctly arrive as `Moved` — only this track misclassifies. Phase should follow the per-track projection: `Began` iff the projection is on this track and the pointer was not already tracked here.
2. Phase is computed from `input.Action == PointerAction.Press` but a pointer may press twice (e.g. Move-after-release resurrects in `PointerInputBuffer.CollectFrame`, which re-adds `_active[input.Id]`) — a repeated press on a pointer that is still in `_mixerTrackPointers` can produce a `Moved`-less `Began` without an intervening release, or conversely a `Moved` for an id that MixerRuntime has already dropped (that is silently ignored by the `!ContainsKey` guard at MixerRuntime.cs:90, so the contact is lost). Sync the set with what was actually emitted (`sample != null && input.Action != Release`) and derive `Began` from track containment only.", "category": "bug", "severity": "high", "suggestion_code": "			var onTrack = sample != null && input.Action != PointerAction.Release;
			var phase = onTrack && !_mixerTrackPointers.Contains(input.Id)
				? ContactPhase.Began : ContactPhase.Moved;"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/gameplay/GameplayMain.Mixer.cs", "existing_code": "			if (input.Action == PointerAction.Release || sample == null) _mixerTrackPointers.Remove(input.Id);
			else _mixerTrackPointers.Add(input.Id);", "content": "Treating `sample == null` on a non-release input as "pointer left the track" causes the very next `Move` for that pointer to be emitted as `Began` again. `MixerRuntime.Apply` then re-runs the catch logic (MixerRuntime.cs:93-94), which can *re-place/transfer* the controller from a position that moved off and back onto the track without the user ever lifting the finger. A pointer that slides off the track should be reported as a contact removal (null position, phase irrelevant), but a subsequent slide back should be a *moved* contact if the runtime still considers it active. Either keep the id tracked while the physical press is active (remove only on Release) and pass `null` position while off-track, or make the `Began` promotion conditional on the runtime not already knowing the pointer.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/gameplay/GameplayMain.Mixer.cs", "existing_code": "		mixer.AdvanceFrame(time, _mixerSeeds, _mixerInputs, _mixerTickTimes,
			(index, grade) => state.MixerGrades[state.MixerTicks[first + index]] = grade, _auto);
		state.MixerTickCursor = next;", "content": "The grade callback indexes `state.MixerTicks[first + index]`, i.e. it assumes `AdvanceFrame` reports each supplied tick as an index into `_mixerTickTimes` (`index == tick` in MixerRuntime.cs:72). That currently holds, but it is an implicit, undocumented contract between this lambda and a shared file that may reorder/dedupe ticks later; additionally `first + index` can silently overflow past `next` (writing a grade for a tick not yet consumed) with no bounds check. Guard the write and make the mapping explicit, e.g. use `_mixerTickTimes[index]` to look up the timestamp and resolve the `JudgeUnit` from it, or assert `first + index < next`. Note also that setting `state.MixerTickCursor = next` unconditionally is only safe while `AdvanceFrame` settles every provided tick; if a future change filters ticks (e.g. `until` clamped by `path.EndTime` at MixerRuntime.cs:45), those ticks are silently skipped forever.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "medium"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/gameplay/GameplayMain.Mixer.cs", "existing_code": "		state.LastContactUpdateTime = mixer.CurrentTime!.Value;", "content": "`mixer.CurrentTime` is `null` until the first `AdvanceFrame` (MixerRuntime.cs:18, set at line 49). It happens to be non-null after a successful `AdvanceFrame(time, ...)` call, but `AdvanceFrame` clamps `until` to `path.EndTime` (line 45) and can throw `ArgumentException` (line 61) for past ticks — in either path the `!` null-forgiving dereference is doing real work and would surface as an NRE rather than a diagnosable error. Prefer an explicit guard: `state.LastContactUpdateTime = Math.Min(t: mixer.CurrentTime ?? state.LastContactUpdateTime, ...)` or skip the assignment when null.", "category": "bug", "severity": "low"}, {"path": "client/scripts/game/gameplay/GameplayMain.Mixer.cs", "existing_code": "		if (time < state.Path.StartTime && !mixer.Placed) return;", "content": "This early return skips both renderer updates (`UpdateMixerHeadView` / `UpdateSustainEffect`) before the mixer is placed. Because `StartResolved` gates entry into `UpdateMixerState` (GameplayMain.Sustain.cs:66), the pre-start window can be entered with `Placed == true` after an `AdvanceFrame` that applied an early placement, but the renderer is not refreshed for a frame where the mixer was *unplaced* at the previous frame — so the head view can stay on screen for one extra frame after the mixer becomes unplaced, and conversely `_noteRenderer` never gets a "hide" call on the pre-start unplaced path. Confirm this is intended; otherwise make the unplaced branch explicitly clear/refresh the head view instead of returning.", "category": "bug", "severity": "low"}]}

{"comments": [{"category": "bug", "content": "_Process is switched off (SetProcess(false)) when the scene settles — here after the enter tween, and in the frozen branch. Nothing guarantees it is switched back on afterwards. ApplyPhase() (called from RestartSampling and from RegisterHit when the phase changes) only mutates state and never calls SetProcess(true) in the non-animated path; the animated path only reaches SetProcess(true) in the initial/not-animated branch. So with an animated motion profile, after the enter animation finished (or after Complete settled) a "再测一次" click leaves the loop stopped: lane dim never animates, hit readout fade never runs, stray readout never expires and the strip median fade never advances. Ensure ApplyPhase/RestartSampling restart processing (e.g. SetProcess(true) at the start of ApplyPhase).", "existing_code": "        _swapPhase = SwapPhase.FadeOut;
        _swapElapsed = 0.0;
        SetGroupAlpha(SwapGroup(), 1f);
        SetProcess(true);", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "high"}, {"category": "bug", "content": "Touch/click dedup: a single touchscreen tap on platforms where touch emulates mouse (Godot `input_devices/pointing/emulate_mouse_from_touch`, enabled by default) delivers both an InputEventScreenTouch and a derived InputEventMouseButton. Since the touch branch returns true but does not mark the viewport handled until after HandleTap, and mouse events are handled here as independent hits, the same physical tap can be registered twice: the second one becomes a `Duplicate`/stray sample and the deviation readout is refreshed twice, corrupting the sample statistics (this is exactly the measurement path the screen exists for). React only to input events that are not emulated touch, e.g. reject `InputEventMouseButton` while `Input.IsEmulatingMouseFromTouch()` / `InputEventMouseButton.Device == -1`, or track finger index.", "existing_code": "        Vector2 position;
        switch (e)
        {
            case InputEventScreenTouch { Pressed: true } touch:
                position = touch.Position;
                break;
            case InputEventMouseButton { ButtonIndex: MouseButton.Left, Pressed: true } click:
                position = click.Position;
                break;
            default:
                return;
        }", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "high"}, {"category": "bug", "content": "RestartSampling changes `_sampling.Phase` back to Ready but never resets `_visualState` nor cancels an in-flight swap. ApplyPhase() only calls SetGroupAlpha on `SwapGroup()` which selects the group by `_visualState`; if a restart (or a phase change) happens while `_swapPhase` is FadeOut/FadeIn, the old group is faded to 0, then CommitPhaseVisuals() flips the visibility and the *new* group is faded — but the previously faded group keeps alpha 0 forever (the fade target group can also be swapped mid-swap since SwapGroup() is re-evaluated each call). Also, since RestartSampling does not reset `_visualState`, a restart while the Complete content is showing can leave the stats/result panel alpha in a stale state. Reset `_swapPhase = SwapPhase.Idle`, `_swapElapsed = 0`, clear modulates of both content roots, and reset `_visualState` before invoking ApplyPhase.", "existing_code": "    private void RestartSampling()
    {
        if (_navigating)
            return;
        _sampling.Restart();
        _lastHitKind = HitReadout.None;
        _strayReadoutRemaining = 0.0;
        _completeElapsed = 0.0;
        ApplyPhase();
    }", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "medium"}, {"category": "bug", "content": "When `complete` is true the strip's samples/median are only updated here (ApplyPhase) and in RefreshReadouts(); after the final Accepted hit RegisterHit calls ApplyPhase() then RefreshReadouts(), so this is normally fine. However, in the frozen branch of _Process the strip is only re-assigned MedianAlpha. The real risk is the freeze path: `_strip.Frozen = true` is set here while `_strip.MedianAlpha = _medianFade` is set to 0 (see the line above, `_medianFade = complete ? 0f : 1f`), and if `SetProcess(false)` was reached before (loop stopped, see the other finding) the MEDIAN line never fades in. Consider not zeroing `_medianFade` here and instead letting _Process own the fade, plus guarantee the process is running.", "existing_code": "        _medianFade = complete ? 0f : 1f;", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "medium"}, {"category": "maintainability", "content": "`_adjacentTargetsEnabled`-style duplication: SetResultActionsEnabled already computes the stepper enabled state, and the two lines right after recompute `_targetMinus/_targetPlus.Disabled` using a slightly different formula (`!ready || _navigating` vs `!enabled || Phase != Ready`). This means SetResultActionsEnabled(false) followed by these lines can silently re-enable the steppers while a navigation is in progress if `_navigating` were false (e.g. ApplyPhase called outside navigation with enabled=false). Additionally, only `.Disabled` on the widgets is updated — the documented "Sampling locks the stepper" is enforced in the model (TrySetTargetSamples) so behaviour is safe, but the duplicated logic is fragile. Fold the stepper gating into a single helper.", "existing_code": "        SetResultActionsEnabled(!_navigating);
        // 步进器只有 Ready 能调：Sampling 锁定、Complete 交给确认面板。
        _targetMinus.Disabled = !ready || _navigating;
        _targetPlus.Disabled = !ready || _navigating;", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "low"}, {"category": "bug", "content": "`_targetMinus`, `_targetPlus` and `_resultSuggestedValue`/`_resultDelta` are dereferenced unconditionally, but `_applyButton`, `_retryButton`, `_discardLabel` are built in BuildResultPanel() before ApplyPhase(initial: true) — verify every field assigned before the first ApplyPhase/RefreshReadouts call. More concretely: RefreshReadouts() reads `_resultSuggestedValue.Text = _suggestedValue.Text;` and `_resultDelta.Text` before `_resultSuggestedValue`/`_resultDelta` may be non-null only if BuildResultPanel() succeeded, but `_strip.Samples`/`_strip.MedianMs` are set twice (ApplyPhase + RefreshReadouts) — the ApplyPhase assignment is redundant since RefreshReadouts always follows immediately (both in _Ready and in RegisterHit). Keep one source of truth to avoid the strip showing stale samples if ApplyPhase is ever called without a follow-up refresh.", "existing_code": "        _strip.Samples = _sampling.Stats.Samples;
        _strip.MedianMs = _sampling.Stats.MedianMs;
        _strip.MedianAlpha = _medianFade;", "path": "client/scripts/ui/CalibrationScreen.cs", "severity": "low"}, {"category": "bug", "content": "Timer-based navigation: `_navigating` and `_localTransitionBusy` are set to true before creating the one-shot timer. If the node leaves the tree (or the timer's SceneTreeTimer is destroyed) before the timeouts, `NavigateToSettings()` never runs and the screen stays permanently busy with all actions disabled — and since the settings were already saved, the user's state is inconsistent. Also the lambda only checks `IsInsideTree()` for navigation but never resets `_localTransitionBusy`/`_navigating` when it does not navigate. Consider using a Tween with a callback tied to the node, and resetting busy flags in `_ExitTree`/on failure.", "existing_code": "        GetTree().CreateTimer(profile.FocusDuration).Timeout += () =>
        {
