{"comments": [{"path": "client/scripts/ui/results/ResultRevealTimeline.cs", "existing_code": "        if (_skipRequested && gradeReady) { Elapsed = Duration; return; }
        var next = Math.Min(Duration, Elapsed + delta);", "content": "The skip request latches forever when the grade never becomes ready (load failure, aborted loader, a `_art.Ready` that never flips). In Full mode `Elapsed` is pinned at `HoldEnd` and `IsComplete` never becomes true, so `ResultScreen` keeps `_actionsEnabled == false` and the screen is permanently stuck: taps are swallowed (the `!IsComplete && pressed` branch calls `SetInputAsHandled()`), no back/retry/next button ever works, and there is no way out except killing the app. The class has no notion of "the asset failed" or a maximum hold. Consider adding an explicit failure/timeout path (e.g. `Fail()` or a `maxHold` that completes the timeline on the still/already-known grade art) so a skip can always terminate the reveal.", "category": "bug", "severity": "high"}, {"path": "client/scripts/ui/results/ResultRevealTimeline.cs", "existing_code": "    public double Opacity(double start, double end) => Mode switch
    {
        UiMotionMode.Off => 1,
        UiMotionMode.Reduced => Smooth(Elapsed / ReducedDuration),
        _ => Smooth((Elapsed - start) / (end - start)),
    };", "content": "The public `Opacity(start, end)` divides by `(end - start)` with no guard. `ResultScreen.RevealGroup` forwards caller-supplied windows, so a zero/negative window (or an accidental `start >= end` after constants are tweaked) yields `NaN`/`+Infinity` from `Smooth`/the division, which then propagates into `Modulate` alpha and can make nodes invisible or log Godot errors. `Smooth`'s clamp does not protect against NaN since `Math.Clamp(NaN, 0, 1)` returns NaN. Guard the degenerate range (return 1 when `end <= start`) so the helper stays total.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium", "suggestion_code": "    public double Opacity(double start, double end) => Mode switch
    {
        UiMotionMode.Off => 1,
        UiMotionMode.Reduced => Smooth(Elapsed / ReducedDuration),
