    {
        if (!double.IsFinite(delta) || delta < 0) return;", "content": "Silently dropping non-finite/negative deltas hides engine clock bugs (e.g. a NaN from an uninitialised timer, or a negative delta from a paused frame) and leaves the timeline frozen with no diagnostics, which is hard to debug in playtests. At minimum emit a debug-only warning (`GD.PushWarning`/`Debug.Assert`) here so the invalid input is observable instead of being swallowed.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "low"}, {"path": "client/scripts/ui/results/ResultRevealTimeline.cs", "existing_code": "        if (_skipRequested && gradeReady) { Elapsed = Duration; return; }", "content": "Once the skip has been honoured, `_skipRequested` is never cleared. It is harmless today because `Elapsed` pins to `Duration` and the screen constructs a fresh timeline per `Present`, but it leaves the object in an inconsistent state (a latched flag that no longer corresponds to a pending request) if the timeline is ever reused or the duration/mode is ever made mutable. Reset the flag when the skip is applied.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "low", "suggestion_code": "        if (_skipRequested && gradeReady) { _skipRequested = false; Elapsed = Duration; return; }"}]}

{"comments": [{"path": "client/scripts/ui/song_select/SongListViewport.cs", "existing_code": "            case InputEventScreenTouch touch when !touch.Pressed && _touchId == touch.Index:
                if (!_touchMoved && _listRect().HasPoint(touch.Position))
                    _selectAt(touch.Position);
                _touchId = null;
                _touchMoved = false;
                GetViewport().SetInputAsHandled();
                return true;", "content": "The release branch does not clear `_scroll.ScrollVertical`-related inertia or re-check the touch start position. More importantly, tap selection is decided solely on the release point: if the press started inside the list but the finger moved <12px and was released over a neighbouring list row/other sibling inside the list rect, `_selectAt` fires for the release position. Since the deadzone is only used to set `_touchMoved`, a borderline drag that never exceeds the deadzone but ends over a different row will select the wrong song. Consider validating that the release point is within the deadzone of `_touchStartY` (or that release position equals start) before selecting.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium"}, {"path": "client/scripts/ui/song_select/SongListViewport.cs", "existing_code": "            case InputEventScreenDrag drag when _touchId == drag.Index:
            {
                var delta = drag.Position.Y - _touchStartY;
