    }", "content": "`_state.Push` uses `_scroll.ScrollVertical` as the actual position, but `MaxScroll()` derives from `bar.MaxValue - bar.Page`. If the scrollbar hasn't been laid out/updated this frame (Page/MaxValue stale), `MaxScroll()` can be below the current `ScrollVertical`, causing `Math.Clamp(actual + pixels, 0, maximum)` in `SongListScrollState.Push` to clamp backwards and snap the list. Additionally, `ScrollVertical` is silently clamped by the ScrollContainer, but `SongListScrollState.Target` isn't told about that until `Step` detects `actual != Written`, which then cancels inertia — so targets beyond the real max produce one frame of "wrong" display followed by a reset. Consider clamping `MaxScroll()` to be at least the current offset, or validating against `bar.MaxValue - bar.Page` only after a layout update.", "category": "bug", "severity": "medium"}, {"path": "client/scripts/ui/song_select/SongListViewport.cs", "existing_code": "    public void Step(double delta)
    {
        if (!_state.Step(_scroll.ScrollVertical, _tau(), delta))
            return;
        Write();
    }", "content": "State tracking relies on `_scroll.ScrollVertical` matching what was last written. If any other code sets `ScrollVertical` (e.g. keyboard navigation, `EnsureControlVisible`, or selection auto-scroll) and then our wheel/drag/touch code runs, `Push` starts from `actual` only when `!Animating`; while animating, `Push` continues from the stale `Target`, so externally written positions are only healed on the next `Step`. This can cause visible snapping after external scroll writes during an animation. Consider centralizing all `ScrollVertical` writes through this class, or having `Push` re-sync when `actual != _state.Written`.", "category": "maintainability", "severity": "low"}, {"path": "client/scripts/ui/song_select/SongListViewport.cs", "existing_code": "    private Viewport GetViewport() => _scroll.GetViewport();", "content": "`GetViewport()` is called on every input event; `ScrollContainer.GetViewport()` can return null while the node is not in the scene tree. The lock above `writer.Write` also suggests `Write()` and `Step()` can be called from different threads, while `_state`, `_touchId` and `_touchMoved` are not synchronized — if both paths can run concurrently (e.g. Step from `_PhysicsProcess` while input comes from a different thread) the animation state can be corrupted. Ensure all accesses happen on the main thread.", "category": "bug", "severity": "low"}]}

{"comments": [{"path": "editor/scripts/authoring/AuthoringCanvasPlacement.cs", "existing_code": "    private void StepInteractionVisual()
