comments.content:
The offset overload allocates the array once and then mutates it in place. That is correct today because `CutCorners` returns a fresh array, but the aliasing contract is implicit and will silently break if `CutCorners` is ever changed to return a cached/shared buffer. Prefer building the offset points directly against an explicit destination to make the ownership obvious:

```csharp
try
{
    var points = CutCorners(new Vector2(width, height), cut);
    var origin = new Vector2(x, y);
    for (var i = 0; i < points.Length; i++)
        points[i] += origin;
    return points;
}
```

At minimum, note in the doc comment that the returned array is a new allocation owned by the caller.

