comments.content:
The 594/12 frame split (and the 100..130 window below, the 300-frame loop in ChartFirstThenAudio) hard-codes 1/60 ≈ MaxStepSeconds/30 against `GameplayEndCondition.MaxStepSeconds`. 594 frames = 9.9 s only because the per-frame delta is `min(1/60, 0.5)` = 1/60. If MaxStepSeconds is ever lowered below 1/60 (a plausible tuning change), every delta gets clamped and these loops silently stop exercising the intended boundary — worse, the negative assertion `!end.ChartEndReached` after 594 frames would *pass* while the positive assertion after 12 more frames would fail, so the failure message points at the wrong thing. Better to derive iterations from the production constants rather than magic numbers, e.g.

```csharp
var step = Math.Min(Frame, GameplayEndCondition.MaxStepSeconds);
var needed = (int)Math.Ceiling((end.ChartEndTime - anchor) / step);
```
and assert with an explicit ±1 frame tolerance instead of hard-coded 594/12/300.

